     The most common uses of TLS will require you to generate a server cer‐
     tificate, which is provided by your host as evidence of its identity when
     clients make new connections.  The certificates reside in the _/_e_t_c_/_s_s_l
     directory, with the keys in the _/_e_t_c_/_s_s_l_/_p_r_i_v_a_t_e directory.

     Private keys can be encrypted using AES and a passphrase to protect their
     integrity should the encrypted file be disclosed.  However, it is impor‐
     tant to note that encrypted server keys mean that the passphrase needs to
     be typed in every time the server is started.  If a passphrase is not
     used, you will need to be absolutely sure your key file is kept secure.

